# Workspace Core Rules

This is a multi-repo orchestration workspace. The full playbook is in `AGENTS.md`; these are the non-negotiables that always apply.

## Repos
- Managed repos are declared in `workspace.json` and cloned as siblings (`../<repo>`). Read `workspace.json` to discover them.
- Use `./scripts/worktree.sh <repo> agent/<task-id>-<desc>` for isolated branches. Never work directly on a repo's default branch.

## Task tracking
- Use Beads (`bd`) when installed; otherwise track work in `TASKS.md`. Check with `command -v bd`.
- Always claim before starting (`bd update <id> --claim`), always close when done (`bd close <id> --reason ...`).
- No dangling tasks, no orphan work (every feature/bug/task is a child of an epic).
- Always pass `--json` to bd commands. Never use `bd edit`.

## Roles
- `role:architect` — planning/epics (the plan skill, run inline).
- `role:coder` — implementation (worker skill / Forge).
- `role:reviewer` — review tasks (reviewer skill / Sentinel).

## Branch naming
```
agent/<task-id>-<short-description>
```

## Pull requests
- Title follows Conventional Commits; include the ticket key when an issue tracker is used: `<type>: description [<TICKET-KEY>]`.
- Follow the target repo's PR template verbatim when one exists.
- Body must include: the spec link on `origin/main` (`https://github.com/<ORG>/<WORKSPACE_REPO>/blob/main/specs/<KEY>.md`), task IDs, and a ticket link if applicable.
- Push the spec to `origin/main` of this workspace before opening any PR that references it.
- The reviewer opens PRs after review passes; the worker never opens PRs.

## Response style
- Don't announce tool calls or narrate intermediate steps; just act.
- Don't editorialize tool output or echo what the user already saw.
- Report outcomes (PR URL, task status), not process logs. No closing pleasantries.
